{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794815619364,"sessionID":"ses_e1ca84ea9ffed5hOlqAimuHTWO","part":{"partID":"prt_1e358f0a0002YdqawOWDvTE0GI","sessionID":"ses_e1ca84ea9ffed5hOlqAimuHTWO","messageID":"msg_1e358dfa10019rUe1hgU1hejW6","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6db963a26bc4133","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff 695f4191d36620b42b5f76a28c25e460fccdd0f7 85550ee946e -- .github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml .skilled/agents/markdown.md .skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/sk-doc/manifest.json .skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown .skilled/hooks/shared/session-readers/README.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md"},"output":"diff --git a/.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml b/.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml\nindex ce53219d72f..521b27cfb5a 100644\n--- a/.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml\n+++ b/.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml\n@@ -20,7 +20,9 @@ on:\n       # The mirror-parity job compares generated mirrors against their sources.\n       # Triggering on the spec-kit runtime alone let a mirror drift on a command\n       # or agent commit, ship unseen, and fail here later on whoever next touched\n-      # the runtime. Every mirror source and every mirror output triggers it now.\n+      # the runtime. Every command and agent mirror source and output triggers it now.\n+      # A Vibe-only or SKILL.md-only change runs the same Vibe checks in\n+      # command-tree-parity.yml, which filters on those paths.\n       - '.opencode/commands/**'\n       - '.skilled/commands/**'\n       - '.opencode/agents/**'\n@@ -65,7 +67,9 @@ on:\n       # The mirror-parity job compares generated mirrors against their sources.\n       # Triggering on the spec-kit runtime alone let a mirror drift on a command\n       # or agent commit, ship unseen, and fail here later on whoever next touched\n-      # the runtime. Every mirror source and every mirror output triggers it now.\n+      # the runtime. Every command and agent mirror source and output triggers it now.\n+      # A Vibe-only or SKILL.md-only change runs the same Vibe checks in\n+      # command-tree-parity.yml, which filters on those paths.\n       - '.opencode/commands/**'\n       - '.skilled/commands/**'\n       - '.opencode/agents/**'\n@@ -346,6 +350,9 @@ jobs:\n           node .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs\n           node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-hook-registrations.cjs --check\n           node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-gate1-pointers.cjs --check\n+          node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/vibe/sync-skills-vibe.cjs --check\n+          node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/vibe/sync-prompts-vibe.cjs --check\n+          node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/vibe/sync-agents-vibe.cjs --check\n \n   doctor-scripts:\n     name: Doctor script test suites\ndiff --git a/.skilled/agents/markdown.md b/.skilled/agents/markdown.md\nindex 898a46d92c5..098ff89ee39 100644\n--- a/.skilled/agents/markdown.md\n+++ b/.skilled/agents/markdown.md\n@@ -194,7 +194,7 @@ Read `sk-doc` first, then the mode packet entry file in the matching row, then t\n | Command                    | Output                                                  | Template                                                                                                                                         |\n | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n | `/create:agent` | New OpenCode agent `.md` plus requested runtime mirrors, and for an agent under `.skilled/agents/` its record in `agent-registry.json` and the regenerated rosters | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-agent/SKILL.md` and the agent template it names |\n-| `/create:skill` | New skill `SKILL.md` or doc-only skill resource | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/SKILL.md` and the skill template it names and, when needed, its skill reference template |\n+| `/create:sk-skill` | New skill `SKILL.md` or doc-only skill resource | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/SKILL.md` and the skill template it names and, when needed, its skill reference template |\n | `/create:skill-parent` | Parent skill with nested mode packets (hub + registry + N `deep-<mode>` packets + `shared/`, one hub `graph-metadata.json`, root `ROUTER.md` stage-two control document) | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/SKILL.md` and that packet's `ROUTER.md`, which names the parent-skill hub, registry and root-router templates (root `ROUTER.md` two-state authoring) |\n | `/create:feature-catalog` | `feature-catalog/` package | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-feature-catalog/SKILL.md` and the catalog template it names |\n | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` | `manual-testing-playbook/` package | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/SKILL.md` and the playbook template it names |\n@@ -287,7 +287,7 @@ If any required check fails, do not return `STATUS=OK`. Return `STATUS=FAIL ERRO\n | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md`                                                    | Required skill routing and documentation creation standards        |\n | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-agent/SKILL.md` | Entry file for `/create:agent`; names the production agent template with its BINDING, REFUSE, and summary contracts |\n-| `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/SKILL.md` | Entry file for `/create:skill` and `/create:skill-parent`; names the skill template and, through the packet's `ROUTER.md`, the root `ROUTER.md` stage-two authoring template |\n+| `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/SKILL.md` | Entry file for `/create:sk-skill` and `/create:skill-parent`; names the skill template and, through the packet's `ROUTER.md`, the root `ROUTER.md` stage-two authoring template |\n | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-feature-catalog/SKILL.md` | Entry file for the feature catalog package template |\n | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/SKILL.md` | Entry file for the manual testing playbook package template |\n | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md` | Entry file for the README template for `/create:readme` |\ndiff --git a/.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/sk-doc/manifest.json b/.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/sk-doc/manifest.json\nindex a2a9bcc5bd7..5a3d1414cca 100644\n--- a/.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/sk-doc/manifest.json\n+++ b/.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/sk-doc/manifest.json\n@@ -1 +1 @@\n-{\"schemaVersion\":\"V1\",\"selectedPolicy\":{\"effectivePolicyHash\":\"97825c55a828ce6763e6900c0dea1d7bd65db495957bd63740fddeeb183d4543\",\"generation\":5},\"servingAuthority\":\"compiled\",\"shadowOnly\":false}\n+{\"schemaVersion\":\"V1\",\"selectedPolicy\":{\"effectivePolicyHash\":\"252cd2b5c4a1afdb20bedefe96ecb671df9ac8b062ddac52fa5643da43c95f86\",\"generation\":5},\"servingAuthority\":\"compiled\",\"shadowOnly\":false}\ndiff --git a/.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md b/.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md\nindex 95842b285a0..502afa0800f 100644\n--- a/.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md\n+++ b/.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md\n@@ -40,8 +40,8 @@ Other gaps cost your agent time. A recommendation said `sk-doc` when you asked f\n - **Install a patched Node.js first.** Node 20, 23 and 25 are no longer accepted. <!-- section: Node.js -->\n - **An agent can no longer read or write `.env`.** It gets key names, parse errors and fingerprints instead, on all eight runtimes. <!-- section: Dotenv Guard -->\n - **A hub skill names the command to type.** The brief also says whether to run it, start it or ask first. <!-- section: Skill Advisor -->\n-- **A typed slash command always reaches the model.** It arrives with its policy and the skill that owns it. <!-- section: Skill Advisor -->\n-- **Doctor checks show whether the brief arrives.** `/doctor:setup` gains an embedder leg and a delivery leg. <!-- section: Skill Advisor -->\n+- **A typed slash command always reaches the model.** It arrives with its policy and the skill that owns it. **Breaking:** `/create:skill` is now `/create:sk-skill`, with no alias. <!-- section: Skill Advisor -->\n+- **Doctor checks show whether the brief arrives.** `/doctor:setup` gains an embedder leg and a delivery leg. Hook health shows with no debug flag. <!-- section: Skill Advisor -->\n - **Claude Code hands over by default.** A hop keeps the running command, the goal, the session's name and its mode. The other runtimes stay off until you turn them on. <!-- section: Session Handover -->\n - **The shell shim runs only a wrapper it trusts.** A cloned repository can no longer supply the script your shell runs. <!-- section: Session Handover -->\n - **One main name now covers the whole feature.** The hook folder, the switches, the seed and the docs say session handover. The in-session spelling still works for the on and off switches. <!-- section: Session Handover -->\n@@ -97,28 +97,28 @@ Claude Code, Devin and Cursor also carry their own deny list for dotenv files. S\n \n #### A Hub Skill Names the Command to Type\n \n-The advisor used to say `sk-doc` for \"create a readme\". You then had to find `/create:readme` yourself. Now a hub recommendation carries the command for the mode it picked. A brief head reads `use system-spec-kit → /speckit:plan (start)`. A hub that cannot pick one mode says so, as `sk-doc (needs a mode)`. A deep-loop command is offered only when the prompt asks for depth.\n+The advisor used to say `sk-doc` for \"create a readme\". You then had to find `/create:readme` yourself. Now a hub recommendation carries the command for the mode it picked. A brief head reads `use system-spec-kit → /speckit:plan (start)`. A hub that cannot pick one mode says so, as `sk-doc (needs a mode)`. It says so only when your prompt names that hub. In the prompt hook, the top hub keeps its command even when a lower row carries the same one. A deep-loop command is offered only when the prompt asks for depth or types that command. A mention of `/deep:review` no longer offers `/deep:research`.\n \n #### Every Command Has a Run, Start or Ask Policy\n \n Each of the 44 tracked commands records what it writes and what it must ask first. The generator turns that record into one policy. `run` means the agent may run it now. `start` means it starts without `:auto`, so its setup asks. `ask` means it offers the command and waits. Gate 2 in `AGENTS.md` now tells the agent how to follow each one. A typed command or a direct instruction outranks the policy.\n \n-A typed slash command now reaches the model on every tracked row, with its policy and owner skill. Plain requests reach their command more often too. Paraphrases the tuning never saw still mostly miss.\n+A typed slash command now reaches the model on every tracked row, with its policy and owner skill. Plain requests reach their command more often too. Paraphrases the tuning never saw still mostly miss. A file name such as `/create:sk-skill.md` is not read as a typed command. A mode suffix such as `/deep:review:auto` still counts. **Breaking:** `/create:skill` is now `/create:sk-skill` on every runtime. A command file named `skill.md` made Claude Code read its folder as one skill. That hid 12 of the 44 commands. No command file answers to the old name. There is no alias. The advisor reads a typed `/create:skill` as `/create:sk-skill`. So that prompt gets `sk-doc` with `/create:sk-skill (start)`. Scripts and saved prompts still need the new name. The runtime mirror check now fails any command file named `skill.md`.\n \n #### Meaning Matching Has Its Own Doctor Route\n \n-A command can also pass on meaning when the local embedder runs. Each scored command row carries its own vector. A prompt needs a close match and a clear lead over the next command. A prompt that asks for no change gets no command this way. `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder` checks the embedder and plans each missing step. It asks before it pulls a model or starts a server. It never installs Ollama itself. The prompt embedding gives up after 750 ms.\n+A command can also pass on meaning when the local embedder runs. Each scored command row carries its own vector. A prompt needs a close match and a clear lead over the next command. A prompt that asks for no change gets no command this way or from a hub route. A command you type still comes through. \"make no changes\" and \"don't make any changes\" both count. `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder` checks the embedder and plans each missing step. It asks before it pulls a model or starts a server. It never installs Ollama itself. The prompt embedding gives up after 750 ms.\n \n #### Doctor Checks Show Whether the Brief Arrives\n \n-`/doctor:setup` gains two read-only legs. The embedder leg reads DEGRADED when meaning matching is off. The delivery leg reads DEGRADED when a prompt hook is missing or not approved. On Codex, no brief arrives until you approve the project hooks. A default `embed` now leaves Claude Code a one-line `CLAUDE.md` pointer to `AGENTS.md`.\n+`/doctor:setup` gains two read-only legs. The embedder leg reads DEGRADED when meaning matching is off. The delivery leg reads DEGRADED when a prompt hook is missing or not approved. On Codex, no brief arrives until you approve the project hooks. A default `embed` now leaves Claude Code a one-line `CLAUDE.md` pointer to `AGENTS.md`. Hook health no longer needs `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG`. Every prompt hook run writes a health record, even a disabled one. It holds the runtime, status, freshness, duration and cache hit. It holds no prompt text. The OpenCode plugin writes none. `advisor_status` then shows a `hookHealth` section. It adds the share of answers from the local scorer and the time of the newest record. The setup advisor leg shows both. The hook-wiring check now reads the command a shell would run. A hook that only names the advisor script reads as missing. The Codex check names the fix for a missing entry.\n \n #### Smaller Fixes\n \n - **A question about the trigger index reaches spec-kit.** It used to end at the doctor helper. A question about index drift still goes to the doctor.\n - **The doctor hand-off names the matched command.** It used to name only `/doctor:helper`, even when the exact command was known.\n - **A prompt that names a `system-`, `cli-` or `mcp-` skill is read.** The prompt policy used to read only `sk-` names.\n-- **An OpenCode brief names the command and its policy.** The plugin used to drop both.\n+- **An OpenCode brief names the command and its policy.** The plugin used to drop both. It also drops a second route line that printed `targets=none`.\n - **Three plain spec requests now reach spec-kit.** The advisor used to miss them.\n - **A read-only question about git history reaches sk-git.** `show the git log` used to return no skill. The question must name git, a commit or one of the branches main, master and develop.\n - **The advisor starts on the Node.js its native module was built for.** An install now records that Node.js. A session on another major version used to fail its first call with `daemon launcher exited with code 1`.\n@@ -477,7 +477,7 @@ The lookup budget is 200 ms at p95. A lookup with no hits takes 89 to 102 ms aga\n - **A live Claude Code hop passed on the renamed code.** It scored 5 of 5 with the seed `[session-handover]`. The note reads done.\n - **A live Mistral Vibe hop completed.** The sender prepared the note. The wrapper printed the claim command. The receiver claimed the note and marked it done. Two later hops on the hardened path scored 5 of 5.\n \n-`system-spec-kit` (v2.12.41.0 to v2.13.2.0) has its own changelog entries for this release. So does `system-skill-advisor` (v0.18.6.0, v0.18.7.0 and v1.0.0.0). So do `system-skilled-doctor` (v1.1.3.0 to v1.1.6.0) and `system-deep-loop` (v1.6.24.0 to v2.0.2.0). Its modes `deep-research` (v1.15.13.0 to v1.15.15.0) and `deep-review` (v1.11.15.0 to v1.11.17.0) have entries. `cli-external-orchestration` (v2.0.0.0) and its modes `cli-opencode` (v1.5.10.0 to v1.5.12.0) and `cli-deepseek-harness` (v1.0.1.0 and v1.0.2.0) have entries too. So does `sk-doc` (v2.9.8.0 to v2.9.15.0). Its modes `sk-create-benchmark` (v1.6.7.0), `sk-create-changelog` (v1.5.0.0 to v1.5.2.0) and `sk-create-feature-catalog` (v1.0.12.0) have entries. So do `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook` (v1.1.2.0) and `sk-create-readme` (v1.3.15.0 and v1.3.16.0). So do `sk-create-repo-rule` (v1.1.8.0) and `sk-create-skill` (v1.16.0.0). So does `sk-create-agent` (v1.0.8.0 to v1.0.10.0). So does `sk-prompt` (v4.0.5.0). `sk-code` (v3.11.5.0 to v3.12.1.0) has entries. So do its modes `sk-code-quality` (v1.13.19.0 to v1.13.21.0) and `sk-code-review` (v1.9.17.0 and v1.9.18.0). So do `sk-code-skilled` (v2.2.0.0 to v2.2.2.0), `sk-code-frontend` (v1.2.0.0) and `sk-code-ci` (v1.1.16.0 and v1.1.17.0). So does `mcp-code-mode` (v1.0.18.0). `mcp-tooling` (v1.9.9.0 to v1.9.16.0) has entries. So does its mode `mcp-click-up` (v1.2.17.0 to v1.2.23.0). Its modes `mcp-chrome-devtools` (v1.0.24.0), `mcp-aside-devtools` (v1.1.10.0) and `mcp-figma` (v1.2.3.0) have entries too.\n+`system-spec-kit` (v2.12.41.0 to v2.13.3.0) has its own changelog entries for this release. So does `system-skill-advisor` (v0.18.6.0, v0.18.7.0, v1.0.0.0 and v1.1.0.0). So do `system-skilled-doctor` (v1.1.3.0 to v1.1.7.0) and `system-deep-loop` (v1.6.24.0 to v2.0.2.0). Its modes `deep-research` (v1.15.13.0 to v1.15.15.0) and `deep-review` (v1.11.15.0 to v1.11.17.0) have entries. `cli-external-orchestration` (v2.0.0.0) and its modes `cli-opencode` (v1.5.10.0 to v1.5.12.0) and `cli-deepseek-harness` (v1.0.1.0 and v1.0.2.0) have entries too. So does `sk-doc` (v2.9.8.0 to v2.10.0.0). Its modes `sk-create-benchmark` (v1.6.7.0), `sk-create-changelog` (v1.5.0.0 to v1.5.2.0) and `sk-create-feature-catalog` (v1.0.12.0) have entries. So do `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook` (v1.1.2.0) and `sk-create-readme` (v1.3.15.0 and v1.3.16.0). So do `sk-create-repo-rule` (v1.1.8.0) and `sk-create-skill` (v1.16.0.0). So does `sk-create-agent` (v1.0.8.0 to v1.0.10.0). So does `sk-prompt` (v4.0.5.0). `sk-code` (v3.11.5.0 to v3.12.1.0) has entries. So do its modes `sk-code-quality` (v1.13.19.0 to v1.13.21.0) and `sk-code-review` (v1.9.17.0 and v1.9.18.0). So do `sk-code-skilled` (v2.2.0.0 to v2.2.2.0), `sk-code-frontend` (v1.2.0.0) and `sk-code-ci` (v1.1.16.0 and v1.1.17.0). So does `mcp-code-mode` (v1.0.18.0). `mcp-tooling` (v1.9.9.0 to v1.9.16.0) has entries. So does its mode `mcp-click-up` (v1.2.17.0 to v1.2.23.0). Its modes `mcp-chrome-devtools` (v1.0.24.0), `mcp-aside-devtools` (v1.1.10.0) and `mcp-figma` (v1.2.3.0) have entries too.\n \n &nbsp;\n \n@@ -487,7 +487,7 @@ The lookup budget is 200 ms at p95. A lookup with no hits takes 89 to 102 ms aga\n - **If an agent needs a `.env` value:** it now asks you. Paste the value yourself or compare fingerprints. For fingerprints, run `openssl rand -base64 32` and export the result as `ENV_SECRET_GUARD_PEPPER` in your shell profile. Never put it in a file inside a repository.\n - **If you embed the shipped `AGENTS.md`:** run `/doctor:agents-md` to take the new Gate 2 and Gate 6 wording. Remove any link of your own to the three retired reply rules.\n - **If you run the skill advisor from a built copy:** run `npm run build` in `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime` once.\n-- **If you match on an advisor command id:** `memory:save` and `command-memory-save` are now `command-speckit-save`. `command-spec-kit` is now one id per command, such as `command-speckit-plan`. `command-prompt-improver` is now `command-prompt-improve`.\n+- **If you type `/create:skill` or match on an advisor command id:** type `/create:sk-skill` instead. Change any script or saved prompt that names the old command. `memory:save` and `command-memory-save` are now `command-speckit-save`. `command-spec-kit` is now one id per command, such as `command-speckit-plan`. `command-prompt-improver` is now `command-prompt-improve`. `command-create-skill` is now `command-create-sk-skill`.\n - **On Codex:** approve the project hooks in `/hooks`, including the new `SessionStart` row. Until then the advisor brief never arrives. The compaction pointer does not run either. To turn on meaning matching anywhere, run `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder`.\n - **If Skilled is installed in your project:** run `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-hook-registrations.cjs` from the project root. It rewrites `.claude/settings.json` and `.codex/hooks.json`. Add `--check` to check for drift without writing.\n - **If you set a `SYSTEM_SESSION_RELAUNCH_*` or `SESSION_RELAUNCH_*` variable:** rename it in your shell profile or `hook-flags.env` by replacing `SESSION_RELAUNCH` with `SESSION_HANDOVER`. For example, `SYSTEM_SESSION_RELAUNCH_ENABLED` becomes `SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_ENABLED`. The old names are not read. A Claude Code user who had switched handover off gets it back until the new switch is set to 0.\ndiff --git a/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown b/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown\nindex c9671f47710..b0d3c68017f 100644\n--- a/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown\n+++ b/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown\n@@ -99,8 +99,8 @@ The scripts validate route manifests, inspect MCP installations, audit parent sk\n | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | The quality config doctor's entry, kept thin: the arguments, the run context, the one call that starts the runner, the mode table and the exit status are here, and each mode sits in the quality modules under `lib/`. It reports which quality config the sk-code quality gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, and the trust state the runner applies to that config: `trusted`, `untrusted` or `none`. `status` and `check` both print it, and when the config is untrusted they print `git config --local skilled.trustRepoChecks true` as the repair. It prints the hygiene layers, a `Hygiene:` line naming the core rules `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off and an applied-rules block built by calling `quality-gate.cjs --explain` over a sample of one file per first three folders and extension: each checklist with its seat, its override rows or the reasons its section was refused, each same-level conflict and a `Hygiene rules:` line for the rules in force. A refused section, an unknown rule id or a missing checklist fails the status with `CONFIG_INVALID` or `CHECKLIST_MISSING`, and a conflict is listed without failing it. It validates that config and creates `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` once from the shipped template for `/doctor:sk-code quality`, and in a repository with no `specs/` folder its plan suggests the spec-kit hygiene rules without writing them. It never runs a command the config names. It exits 0 on success, 1 on an invalid or missing config, a refused checklist section or a refused init and 2 on a usage error or outside a git repository. |\n | `audit_descriptions.py` | Audits description lengths across skills, commands and agents. |\n | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Verifies every agent in `.skilled/agents` reaches all eight runtime trees, and that the Cursor and Devin entries stay symlinked onto the Claude fork rather than forked. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n-| `agents-md-check.cjs` | The read-only AGENTS.md check: twelve rows reporting the install state, the sections lost or reordered, the currency of each body, every pin, the delivery budget, the shape of the repository block, the repository's own content and its local edits, each runtime copy, the `REPO RULES.md` router and the source template's purity. Three of them run their owner as a child instead of re-deriving it, carrying the rule-copy canary's budget lines, the gate 1 pointer sync's Cursor block and the repo-rules checker's summary. `--root <dir>` classifies another repository and `--json` prints the same report for a machine. A row it cannot prove reads `UNKNOWN` rather than `PASS`, so it exits 0 on no finding, 1 on a finding or an unproven row and 2 on a failed precondition. |\n-| `agents-md-embed.cjs` | The writing half of `/doctor:agents-md`, in three subcommands. `plan` composes every target in memory and prints the targets, the diffs, the new block, the byte budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest, and writes nothing. `apply --plan-digest <digest>` recomposes, refuses a digest the tree no longer produces, backs up every path it will touch, writes only inside the allowlist it checks in code and then proves the composition against the bytes on disk, restoring from the backup and ending `STATUS=FAIL` when one line went missing. `restore --run=<dir>` puts back exactly the paths that run's `manifest.json` records, removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created, and keeps the backup. It exits 0 when there was nothing to do or the work finished, 1 on a failed proof or a declined run and 2 on a failed precondition or a path outside the allowlist. |\n+| `agents-md-check.cjs` | The read-only AGENTS.md check: twelve rows reporting the install state, the sections lost or reordered, the currency of each body, every pin, the delivery budget, the shape of the repository block, the repository's own content and its local edits, each runtime copy, the `REPO RULES.md` router and the source template's purity. Four of them run their owner as a child instead of re-deriving it, carrying the rule-copy canary's budget lines, the gate 1 pointer sync's Cursor block, the repo-rules checker's summary and the manifest generator's check of the source template's purity. `--root <dir>` classifies another repository and `--json` prints the same report for a machine. A row it cannot prove reads `UNKNOWN` rather than `PASS`, so it exits 0 on no finding, 1 on a finding or an unproven row and 2 on a failed precondition. |\n+| `agents-md-embed.cjs` | The writing half of `/doctor:agents-md`, in three subcommands. `plan` composes every target in memory and prints the targets, the diffs, the new block, the byte budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest, and writes nothing. `apply --plan-digest <digest>` recomposes, refuses a digest the tree no longer produces, backs up every path it will touch, writes only inside the allowlist it checks in code and then proves the composition against the bytes on disk, restoring from the backup and ending `STATUS=FAIL` when one line went missing or one appeared outside the block that no shipped section carries. It refuses a symlinked `REPO RULES.md` or Cursor mirror it would write, because a write through the link would change a file the repository does not own. `restore --run=<dir>` puts back exactly the paths that run's `manifest.json` records, removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created, and keeps the backup. It exits 0 when there was nothing to do or the work finished, 1 on a failed proof or a declined run and 2 on a failed precondition or a path outside the allowlist. It exits 3 when a write fails after the backup, having restored from it or said why not. |\n | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | Generates and gates the shipped manifest's `derived` key: a copy of every shipped body, its hash and the router rows, taken from the root documents. `--write` refreshes it and `--check` is the freshness gate, so a body that drifted from the template fails here instead of surfacing as a check finding. It refuses a heading no authored entry matches, an authored entry that matches nothing, and text above the first heading, and `--write` runs only in the repository that ships the template. Each of the three engines has its own suite: `agents-md-check.test.cjs`, `agents-md-embed.test.cjs` and `agents-md-manifest.test.cjs`, driven from the recipe folders under `tests/fixtures/agents-md/`. |\n | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | Lists the shipped hook gates from `scripts/git-hooks/lib/gates.tsv` with their saved `speckit.hooks.<key>` values, and sets or unsets one in local or global git config for `/doctor:git hooks`. Refuses the per-push approvals. |\n | `git-standards.cjs` | Shows where a repository's commit, PR and branch rules come from, copies the shipped sk-git templates into `.sk-git/`, and changes a rules block setting or removes a kind's rules section in those copies for `/doctor:git standards`, re-validating each change with sk-git's own contract check. |\n@@ -109,7 +109,7 @@ The scripts validate route manifests, inspect MCP installations, audit parent sk\n | `doctor-helper-match.cjs` | Ranks the doctor commands of `_routes.yaml` against a problem stated in plain words for `/doctor:helper`: one entry per route, standalone command, standalone action and mcp sub-action, scored by inverse-document-frequency overlap over the manifest phrases plus a bonus for a fully covered phrase, with a preference for the mutation class the operator asked for. Each candidate carries the manifest phrase that best matched and the terms it matched, so the reason on every line is evidence. It skips its own route and every private name, reads one YAML file and writes nothing. It exits 0 on a match, 1 on no candidate above the floor and 2 on a bad argument, an unreadable manifest or a missing YAML parser. |\n | `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` | Scores every activated doctor phrase and each runnable doctor command through the built advisor scorer, then keeps the three results the hooks show. It fails when a phrase reaches neither the top pick nor the second line. It also fails if a runnable command does not rank first or an activated phrase has fewer than three words. It exits 2 when the advisor build is missing or stale. |\n | `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` | The read-only Codex approval check: it finds the advisor's `UserPromptSubmit` entry in `.codex/hooks.json` and reports whether the Codex config holds a `trusted_hash` for that exact group and index, because Codex skips an unapproved entry without a warning. `--root <dir>` reads another checkout, `--config <path>` reads another config and `--json` prints one object. It prints `SKIP no-codex-hooks`, `PASS codex-hooks-approved`, `WARN codex-advisor-hook-absent` or `WARN codex-hooks-unapproved <root>` with the `/hooks` repair line, and never prints a hash. It exits 0 on a skip or an approval, 1 on a missing or unapproved entry and 2 on a bad argument. |\n-| `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | The read-only hook-wiring check: it reads `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json`, `.cursor/hooks.json` and `.devin/hooks.v1.json`, and reports whether the prompt hook entry is present for each runtime folder it finds. `--root <dir>` is required and `--json` prints one object. It prints `ok <runtime>` or `hook-missing <runtime> <file>`, skips a runtime whose folder is absent and never writes. It exits 0 when every installed runtime is wired, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. |\n+| `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | The read-only hook-wiring check: it reads `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json`, `.cursor/hooks.json`, `.devin/hooks.v1.json` and `.vibe/hooks.toml`, and reports whether the prompt hook entry is wired for each runtime folder it finds. An entry counts as wired only when one of its commands, read the way a shell reads it, starts node on that runtime's hook script, so a script named in an `echo` or a comment does not count. It reads `.vibe/hooks.toml` line by line for a `pre_agent` entry that runs the Vibe turn-start hook, because the repository installs no TOML parser. Pi, DSH and OpenCode load their prompt hook through an extension or a plugin, so the check has no row for them. `--root <dir>` is required and `--json` prints one object. It prints `ok <runtime>` or `hook-missing <runtime> <file>`, skips a runtime whose folder is absent and never writes. It exits 0 when every installed runtime is wired, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. |\n | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Checks this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. On 1 it proves plugin discovery through `debug config` and plugin load through an import check. On 2 it starts and stops a private server with the v2 binary it selected (`$OPENCODE_BIN`, `opencode` or `opencode2`, whichever reports a v2 version) and reads plugin and skill state from it. It exits 0 when every requested leg passes, 1 on a failed leg, 2 on a usage error, a crash or an `--out` file it cannot write and 3 when a leg is degraded or skipped. |\n | `ci-report.cjs` | The `/doctor:ci report` entry, kept thin: the flags, the exit codes and every name `/doctor:ci toggle` reads live here, and the readings live in the `ci-report` modules under `lib/`. It reads every workflow under `.github/workflows/` and reports its switch state, triggers, path filters and drift flags for `/doctor:ci report`. A switch is any variable a job's `if:` compares against `off`, so the workflow file is its only copy, and a repository variable value is reduced to a state and never printed. The branch, the required switch, the exempt files, the README and the rebuild trailer come from the CI profile, and the JSON names the profile it read. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 when a workflow fails to parse, 2 on a bad argument and 3 when the CI profile is missing or invalid. |\n | `ci-scaffold.cjs` | Renders the sk-code-ci workflow or job template from the CI profile for `/doctor:ci scaffold`, and with `--protected` prints the profile's protected workflow names. It writes nothing. |\n@@ -232,7 +232,7 @@ On this repository all legs pass on OpenCode 1.19.3 and 2.0.32. OpenCode 1.19.3\n | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | `plan` is read-only. `apply` writes only the paths in its own allowlist, all of them under the target root, after backing each one up under `.skilled/output/doctor-agents-md/<digest>/` (or the OS temp folder when that path is not ignored), and restores from that backup itself when its post-write proof fails. `restore` writes only the paths the run's `manifest.json` records and keeps the backup folder. It never runs `git add`, `git commit` or `git stash`. |\n | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | `--check` is read-only. `--write` rewrites the manifest's `derived` key and refuses to run outside the repository that ships the template. |\n | `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root. It reads `.codex/hooks.json` and the Codex config, prints no hash and takes no write path. It exits 0 on a skip or an approval, 1 on a missing or unapproved entry and 2 on a bad argument. |\n-| `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root. It writes no file and runs no installer, and its JSON form reports the same readings as its lines. It exits 0 when every installed runtime carries its entry, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. |\n+| `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root. It writes no file and runs no installer, and its JSON form reports the same readings as its lines. It exits 0 when every installed runtime's entry runs its hook script, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. Its Vibe reading is line by line, so a `.vibe/hooks.toml` that Vibe cannot parse can still read `ok vibe`, and a value with a trailing comment or a single-quoted string reads `hook-missing`. |\n | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | The battery itself writes only the `--out` file and temporary folders it removes when each step ends. The private server's folder is also removed on `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM`, and the server is stopped. The import check runs each plugin's v1 factory in a child process rooted at the repository, so anything a plugin does when it loads happens during the check. It never touches the shared v2 service and never prints the private server's password. OpenCode 1 writes into a temporary data folder, not the user's. |\n \n Do not invoke a mutating path from a route classified as read-only.\ndiff --git a/.skilled/hooks/shared/session-readers/README.md b/.skilled/hooks/shared/session-readers/README.md\nindex 586fa23f85f..bc58368c3f2 100644\n--- a/.skilled/hooks/shared/session-readers/README.md\n+++ b/.skilled/hooks/shared/session-readers/README.md\n@@ -25,15 +25,16 @@ The six decision-capture sweeps keep their own inline readers and remain the wri\n \n - **Exact id only.** A caller names a runtime and one session id. No reader guesses the newest session or lists a store.\n - **Read-only.** Files are read and SQLite stores are opened with read-only flags. The side-effect suite checks that no read changes a store's bytes or modification time.\n+- **Contained open.** A reader resolves its store path against the runtime's root before opening it, and opens the path its containment check resolved, so a path swapped after the check is not followed.\n - **One envelope.** `readSession` returns `{ ok: true, source, items }` or `{ ok: false, code }`. It never throws across the boundary and never returns a partial list.\n - **Capability is the caller's gate.** `index.mjs` exports `capabilities`, one row per runtime with its `status`, locator kind and store root, plus a `reason` when the runtime is gated. The dispatcher does not consult it. The compaction hooks and the lookup CLI check that a runtime is `ready` before they offer or read history.\n \n-`loadSession` returns the raw neutral records. Claude, Pi, Devin and DSH also return `injected`, a list of `{ source, text }` for context a hook or harness put into the session, which `records` never carries. `loadSession` passes `injected` through and `readSession` ignores it, so only a caller on the raw path sees what the harness added.\n+`loadSession` returns the raw neutral records. Claude, Pi, Devin and DSH also return `injected`, a list of `{ source, text }` for context a hook or harness put into the session, which `records` never carries. `loadSession` passes `injected` through and `readSession` ignores it, so only a caller on the raw path sees what the harness added. A Codex custom tool call has no JSON arguments, so its record carries the call's body as `arguments.input`, a string.\n \n - Claude's `injected` source is `hook_additional_context`, one entry per hook context attachment.\n - Pi's `injected` source is the entry's custom type, such as `session-start-context`, or `custom_message` when it carries none.\n - Devin's `injected` source is `system` for a harness node and `unflagged-user` for a user node the harness wrote. The transcript fallback uses the step's own source.\n-- DSH's `injected` source is the event's source kind, such as `repo-guards`, or `unknown` when it names none.\n+- DSH's `injected` source is the event's source kind, such as `repo-guards`, or `unknown` when it names none. A `user/message` with no source stays injected and never becomes a record.\n \n Failure codes:\n \n@@ -44,7 +45,7 @@ Failure codes:\n | `session_not_found` | No store record matches the id |\n | `session_mismatch` | A record was found, but its recorded id or path does not match |\n | `session_ambiguous` | More than one record claims the id |\n-| `store_unreadable` | The store could not be read or projected |\n+| `store_unreadable` | The store could not be read or projected. For Devin, an open or projection failure falls back to the transcript first, so the code means the transcript failed too |\n | `unsupported_format` | The journal holds no record shape the reader knows, so it fails instead of returning an empty session |\n \n Vibe is gated. Its reader projects `harness.public-session-state/v1` and the completion records beside it, and its helpers are `loadVibeSession`, `lastAssistantText`, `turnToolCalls`, `compactionsSince` and `turnId`. The reply is read from the current turn's completion record first, because Vibe runs a `post_agent` hook before it appends the assistant message to the projection. A hook passes the payload's `transcript_path` (the session storage root); the journal is `<transcript_path>/journal/*.jsonl`. An interactive session folds its journal into a snapshot generation at each quiet point and restarts the journal after it, so the reader starts from the generation `CURRENT` names, its projection chunks under `chunks/`, and applies the journal tail after that generation's `snapshot_sequence`. A compaction folded into a snapshot carries the snapshot's sequence.\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md b/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\nindex fa40840eef3..b22dee1e4e1 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\n@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n name: system-spec-kit\n description: \"Spec-folder workflow + context preservation: Levels 1-3+, validation, trigger-index, ripgrep retrieval. Required for file changes.\"\n allowed-tools: [Bash, Edit, Glob, Grep, Read, Task, Write]\n-version: 2.13.3.0\n+version: 2.13.4.0\n ---\n \n <!-- Keywords: spec-kit, speckit, documentation-workflow, spec-folder, template-enforcement, context-preservation, progressive-documentation, validation, trigger-index, retrieval-conventions, ripgrep-retrieval, continuity-writer, handover, opencode-goal, goal-plugin, active_goal, session-goal, importance-tiers -->\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"diff --git a/.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml b/.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml\nindex ce53219d72f..521b27cfb5a 100644\n--- a/.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml\n+++ b/.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml\n@@ -20,7 +20,9 @@ on:\n       # The mirror-parity job compares generated mirrors against their sources.\n       # Triggering on the spec-kit runtime alone let a mirror drift on a command\n       # or agent commit, ship unseen, and fail here later on whoever next touched\n-      # the runtime. Every mirror source and every mirror output triggers it now.\n+      # the runtime. Every command and agent mirror source and output triggers it now.\n+      # A Vibe-only or SKILL.md-only change runs the same Vibe checks in\n+      # command-tree-parity.yml, which filters on those paths.\n       - '.opencode/commands/**'\n       - '.skilled/commands/**'\n       - '.opencode/agents/**'\n@@ -65,7 +67,9 @@ on:\n       # The mirror-parity job compares generated mirrors against their sources.\n       # Triggering on the spec-kit runtime alone let a mirror drift on a command\n       # or agent commit, ship unseen, and fail here later on whoever next touched\n-      # the runtime. Every mirror source and every mirror output triggers it now.\n+      # the runtime. Every command and agent mirror source and output triggers it now.\n+      # A Vibe-only or SKILL.md-only change runs the same Vibe checks in\n+      # command-tree-parity.yml, which filters on those paths.\n       - '.opencode/commands/**'\n       - '.skilled/commands/**'\n       - '.opencode/agents/**'\n@@ -346,6 +350,9 @@ jobs:\n           node .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs\n           node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-hook-registrations.cjs --check\n           node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-gate1-pointers.cjs --check\n+          node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/vibe/sync-skills-vibe.cjs --check\n+          node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/vibe/sync-prompts-vibe.cjs --check\n+          node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/vibe/sync-agents-vibe.cjs --check\n \n   doctor-scripts:\n     name: Doctor script test suites\ndiff --git a/.skilled/agents/markdown.md b/.skilled/agents/markdown.md\nindex 898a46d92c5..098ff89ee39 100644\n--- a/.skilled/agents/markdown.md\n+++ b/.skilled/agents/markdown.md\n@@ -194,7 +194,7 @@ Read `sk-doc` first, then the mode packet entry file in the matching row, then t\n | Command                    | Output                                                  | Template                                                                                                                                         |\n | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n | `/create:agent` | New OpenCode agent `.md` plus requested runtime mirrors, and for an agent under `.skilled/agents/` its record in `agent-registry.json` and the regenerated rosters | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-agent/SKILL.md` and the agent template it names |\n-| `/create:skill` | New skill `SKILL.md` or doc-only skill resource | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/SKILL.md` and the skill template it names and, when needed, its skill reference template |\n+| `/create:sk-skill` | New skill `SKILL.md` or doc-only skill resource | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/SKILL.md` and the skill template it names and, when needed, its skill reference template |\n | `/create:skill-parent` | Parent skill with nested mode packets (hub + registry + N `deep-<mode>` packets + `shared/`, one hub `graph-metadata.json`, root `ROUTER.md` stage-two control document) | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/SKILL.md` and that packet's `ROUTER.md`, which names the parent-skill hub, registry and root-router templates (root `ROUTER.md` two-state authoring) |\n | `/create:feature-catalog` | `feature-catalog/` package | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-feature-catalog/SKILL.md` and the catalog template it names |\n | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` | `manual-testing-playbook/` package | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/SKILL.md` and the playbook template it names |\n@@ -287,7 +287,7 @@ If any required check fails, do not return `STATUS=OK`. Return `STATUS=FAIL ERRO\n | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md`                                                    | Required skill routing and documentation creation standards        |\n | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-agent/SKILL.md` | Entry file for `/create:agent`; names the production agent template with its BINDING, REFUSE, and summary contracts |\n-| `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/SKILL.md` | Entry file for `/create:skill` and `/create:skill-parent`; names the skill template and, through the packet's `ROUTER.md`, the root `ROUTER.md` stage-two authoring template |\n+| `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/SKILL.md` | Entry file for `/create:sk-skill` and `/create:skill-parent`; names the skill template and, through the packet's `ROUTER.md`, the root `ROUTER.md` stage-two authoring template |\n | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-feature-catalog/SKILL.md` | Entry file for the feature catalog package template |\n | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/SKILL.md` | Entry file for the manual testing playbook package template |\n | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md` | Entry file for the README template for `/create:readme` |\ndiff --git a/.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/sk-doc/manifest.json b/.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/sk-doc/manifest.json\nindex a2a9bcc5bd7..5a3d1414cca 100644\n--- a/.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/sk-doc/manifest.json\n+++ b/.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/sk-doc/manifest.json\n@@ -1 +1 @@\n-{\"schemaVersion\":\"V1\",\"selectedPolicy\":{\"effectivePolicyHash\":\"97825c55a828ce6763e6900c0dea1d7bd65db495957bd63740fddeeb183d4543\",\"generation\":5},\"servingAuthority\":\"compiled\",\"shadowOnly\":false}\n+{\"schemaVersion\":\"V1\",\"selectedPolicy\":{\"effectivePolicyHash\":\"252cd2b5c4a1afdb20bedefe96ecb671df9ac8b062ddac52fa5643da43c95f86\",\"generation\":5},\"servingAuthority\":\"compiled\",\"shadowOnly\":false}\ndiff --git a/.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md b/.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md\nindex 95842b285a0..502afa0800f 100644\n--- a/.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md\n+++ b/.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md\n@@ -40,8 +40,8 @@ Other gaps cost your agent time. A recommendation said `sk-doc` when you asked f\n - **Install a patched Node.js first.** Node 20, 23 and 25 are no longer accepted. <!-- section: Node.js -->\n - **An agent can no longer read or write `.env`.** It gets key names, parse errors and fingerprints instead, on all eight runtimes. <!-- section: Dotenv Guard -->\n - **A hub skill names the command to type.** The brief also says whether to run it, start it or ask first. <!-- section: Skill Advisor -->\n-- **A typed slash command always reaches the model.** It arrives with its policy and the skill that owns it. <!-- section: Skill Advisor -->\n-- **Doctor checks show whether the brief arrives.** `/doctor:setup` gains an embedder leg and a delivery leg. <!-- section: Skill Advisor -->\n+- **A typed slash command always reaches the model.** It arrives with its policy and the skill that owns it. **Breaking:** `/create:skill` is now `/create:sk-skill`, with no alias. <!-- section: Skill Advisor -->\n+- **Doctor checks show whether the brief arrives.** `/doctor:setup` gains an embedder leg and a delivery leg. Hook health shows with no debug flag. <!-- section: Skill Advisor -->\n - **Claude Code hands over by default.** A hop keeps the running command, the goal, the session's name and its mode. The other runtimes stay off until you turn them on. <!-- section: Session Handover -->\n - **The shell shim runs only a wrapper it trusts.** A cloned repository can no longer supply the script your shell runs. <!-- section: Session Handover -->\n - **One main name now covers the whole feature.** The hook folder, the switches, the seed and the docs say session handover. The in-session spelling still works for the on and off switches. <!-- section: Session Handover -->\n@@ -97,28 +97,28 @@ Claude Code, Devin and Cursor also carry their own deny list for dotenv files. S\n \n #### A Hub Skill Names the Command to Type\n \n-The advisor used to say `sk-doc` for \"create a readme\". You then had to find `/create:readme` yourself. Now a hub recommendation carries the command for the mode it picked. A brief head reads `use system-spec-kit → /speckit:plan (start)`. A hub that cannot pick one mode says so, as `sk-doc (needs a mode)`. A deep-loop command is offered only when the prompt asks for depth.\n+The advisor used to say `sk-doc` for \"create a readme\". You then had to find `/create:readme` yourself. Now a hub recommendation carries the command for the mode it picked. A brief head reads `use system-spec-kit → /speckit:plan (start)`. A hub that cannot pick one mode says so, as `sk-doc (needs a mode)`. It says so only when your prompt names that hub. In the prompt hook, the top hub keeps its command even when a lower row carries the same one. A deep-loop command is offered only when the prompt asks for depth or types that command. A mention of `/deep:review` no longer offers `/deep:research`.\n \n #### Every Command Has a Run, Start or Ask Policy\n \n Each of the 44 tracked commands records what it writes and what it must ask first. The generator turns that record into one policy. `run` means the agent may run it now. `start` means it starts without `:auto`, so its setup asks. `ask` means it offers the command and waits. Gate 2 in `AGENTS.md` now tells the agent how to follow each one. A typed command or a direct instruction outranks the policy.\n \n-A typed slash command now reaches the model on every tracked row, with its policy and owner skill. Plain requests reach their command more often too. Paraphrases the tuning never saw still mostly miss.\n+A typed slash command now reaches the model on every tracked row, with its policy and owner skill. Plain requests reach their command more often too. Paraphrases the tuning never saw still mostly miss. A file name such as `/create:sk-skill.md` is not read as a typed command. A mode suffix such as `/deep:review:auto` still counts. **Breaking:** `/create:skill` is now `/create:sk-skill` on every runtime. A command file named `skill.md` made Claude Code read its folder as one skill. That hid 12 of the 44 commands. No command file answers to the old name. There is no alias. The advisor reads a typed `/create:skill` as `/create:sk-skill`. So that prompt gets `sk-doc` with `/create:sk-skill (start)`. Scripts and saved prompts still need the new name. The runtime mirror check now fails any command file named `skill.md`.\n \n #### Meaning Matching Has Its Own Doctor Route\n \n-A command can also pass on meaning when the local embedder runs. Each scored command row carries its own vector. A prompt needs a close match and a clear lead over the next command. A prompt that asks for no change gets no command this way. `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder` checks the embedder and plans each missing step. It asks before it pulls a model or starts a server. It never installs Ollama itself. The prompt embedding gives up after 750 ms.\n+A command can also pass on meaning when the local embedder runs. Each scored command row carries its own vector. A prompt needs a close match and a clear lead over the next command. A prompt that asks for no change gets no command this way or from a hub route. A command you type still comes through. \"make no changes\" and \"don't make any changes\" both count. `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder` checks the embedder and plans each missing step. It asks before it pulls a model or starts a server. It never installs Ollama itself. The prompt embedding gives up after 750 ms.\n \n #### Doctor Checks Show Whether the Brief Arrives\n \n-`/doctor:setup` gains two read-only legs. The embedder leg reads DEGRADED when meaning matching is off. The delivery leg reads DEGRADED when a prompt hook is missing or not approved. On Codex, no brief arrives until you approve the project hooks. A default `embed` now leaves Claude Code a one-line `CLAUDE.md` pointer to `AGENTS.md`.\n+`/doctor:setup` gains two read-only legs. The embedder leg reads DEGRADED when meaning matching is off. The delivery leg reads DEGRADED when a prompt hook is missing or not approved. On Codex, no brief arrives until you approve the project hooks. A default `embed` now leaves Claude Code a one-line `CLAUDE.md` pointer to `AGENTS.md`. Hook health no longer needs `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG`. Every prompt hook run writes a health record, even a disabled one. It holds the runtime, status, freshness, duration and cache hit. It holds no prompt text. The OpenCode plugin writes none. `advisor_status` then shows a `hookHealth` section. It adds the share of answers from the local scorer and the time of the newest record. The setup advisor leg shows both. The hook-wiring check now reads the command a shell would run. A hook that only names the advisor script reads as missing. The Codex check names the fix for a missing entry.\n \n #### Smaller Fixes\n \n - **A question about the trigger index reaches spec-kit.** It used to end at the doctor helper. A question about index drift still goes to the doctor.\n - **The doctor hand-off names the matched command.** It used to name only `/doctor:helper`, even when the exact command was known.\n - **A prompt that names a `system-`, `cli-` or `mcp-` skill is read.** The prompt policy used to read only `sk-` names.\n-- **An OpenCode brief names the command and its policy.** The plugin used to drop both.\n+- **An OpenCode brief names the command and its policy.** The plugin used to drop both. It also drops a second route line that printed `targets=none`.\n - **Three plain spec requests now reach spec-kit.** The advisor used to miss them.\n - **A read-only question about git history reaches sk-git.** `show the git log` used to return no skill. The question must name git, a commit or one of the branches main, master and develop.\n - **The advisor starts on the Node.js its native module was built for.** An install now records that Node.js. A session on another major version used to fail its first call with `daemon launcher exited with code 1`.\n@@ -477,7 +477,7 @@ The lookup budget is 200 ms at p95. A lookup with no hits takes 89 to 102 ms aga\n - **A live Claude Code hop passed on the renamed code.** It scored 5 of 5 with the seed `[session-handover]`. The note reads done.\n - **A live Mistral Vibe hop completed.** The sender prepared the note. The wrapper printed the claim command. The receiver claimed the note and marked it done. Two later hops on the hardened path scored 5 of 5.\n \n-`system-spec-kit` (v2.12.41.0 to v2.13.2.0) has its own changelog entries for this release. So does `system-skill-advisor` (v0.18.6.0, v0.18.7.0 and v1.0.0.0). So do `system-skilled-doctor` (v1.1.3.0 to v1.1.6.0) and `system-deep-loop` (v1.6.24.0 to v2.0.2.0). Its modes `deep-research` (v1.15.13.0 to v1.15.15.0) and `deep-review` (v1.11.15.0 to v1.11.17.0) have entries. `cli-external-orchestration` (v2.0.0.0) and its modes `cli-opencode` (v1.5.10.0 to v1.5.12.0) and `cli-deepseek-harness` (v1.0.1.0 and v1.0.2.0) have entries too. So does `sk-doc` (v2.9.8.0 to v2.9.15.0). Its modes `sk-create-benchmark` (v1.6.7.0), `sk-create-changelog` (v1.5.0.0 to v1.5.2.0) and `sk-create-feature-catalog` (v1.0.12.0) have entries. So do `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook` (v1.1.2.0) and `sk-create-readme` (v1.3.15.0 and v1.3.16.0). So do `sk-create-repo-rule` (v1.1.8.0) and `sk-create-skill` (v1.16.0.0). So does `sk-create-agent` (v1.0.8.0 to v1.0.10.0). So does `sk-prompt` (v4.0.5.0). `sk-code` (v3.11.5.0 to v3.12.1.0) has entries. So do its modes `sk-code-quality` (v1.13.19.0 to v1.13.21.0) and `sk-code-review` (v1.9.17.0 and v1.9.18.0). So do `sk-code-skilled` (v2.2.0.0 to v2.2.2.0), `sk-code-frontend` (v1.2.0.0) and `sk-code-ci` (v1.1.16.0 and v1.1.17.0). So does `mcp-code-mode` (v1.0.18.0). `mcp-tooling` (v1.9.9.0 to v1.9.16.0) has entries. So does its mode `mcp-click-up` (v1.2.17.0 to v1.2.23.0). Its modes `mcp-chrome-devtools` (v1.0.24.0), `mcp-aside-devtools` (v1.1.10.0) and `mcp-figma` (v1.2.3.0) have entries too.\n+`system-spec-kit` (v2.12.41.0 to v2.13.3.0) has its own changelog entries for this release. So does `system-skill-advisor` (v0.18.6.0, v0.18.7.0, v1.0.0.0 and v1.1.0.0). So do `system-skilled-doctor` (v1.1.3.0 to v1.1.7.0) and `system-deep-loop` (v1.6.24.0 to v2.0.2.0). Its modes `deep-research` (v1.15.13.0 to v1.15.15.0) and `deep-review` (v1.11.15.0 to v1.11.17.0) have entries. `cli-external-orchestration` (v2.0.0.0) and its modes `cli-opencode` (v1.5.10.0 to v1.5.12.0) and `cli-deepseek-harness` (v1.0.1.0 and v1.0.2.0) have entries too. So does `sk-doc` (v2.9.8.0 to v2.10.0.0). Its modes `sk-create-benchmark` (v1.6.7.0), `sk-create-changelog` (v1.5.0.0 to v1.5.2.0) and `sk-create-feature-catalog` (v1.0.12.0) have entries. So do `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook` (v1.1.2.0) and `sk-create-readme` (v1.3.15.0 and v1.3.16.0). So do `sk-create-repo-rule` (v1.1.8.0) and `sk-create-skill` (v1.16.0.0). So does `sk-create-agent` (v1.0.8.0 to v1.0.10.0). So does `sk-prompt` (v4.0.5.0). `sk-code` (v3.11.5.0 to v3.12.1.0) has entries. So do its modes `sk-code-quality` (v1.13.19.0 to v1.13.21.0) and `sk-code-review` (v1.9.17.0 and v1.9.18.0). So do `sk-code-skilled` (v2.2.0.0 to v2.2.2.0), `sk-code-frontend` (v1.2.0.0) and `sk-code-ci` (v1.1.16.0 and v1.1.17.0). So does `mcp-code-mode` (v1.0.18.0). `mcp-tooling` (v1.9.9.0 to v1.9.16.0) has entries. So does its mode `mcp-click-up` (v1.2.17.0 to v1.2.23.0). Its modes `mcp-chrome-devtools` (v1.0.24.0), `mcp-aside-devtools` (v1.1.10.0) and `mcp-figma` (v1.2.3.0) have entries too.\n \n &nbsp;\n \n@@ -487,7 +487,7 @@ The lookup budget is 200 ms at p95. A lookup with no hits takes 89 to 102 ms aga\n - **If an agent needs a `.env` value:** it now asks you. Paste the value yourself or compare fingerprints. For fingerprints, run `openssl rand -base64 32` and export the result as `ENV_SECRET_GUARD_PEPPER` in your shell profile. Never put it in a file inside a repository.\n - **If you embed the shipped `AGENTS.md`:** run `/doctor:agents-md` to take the new Gate 2 and Gate 6 wording. Remove any link of your own to the three retired reply rules.\n - **If you run the skill advisor from a built copy:** run `npm run build` in `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime` once.\n-- **If you match on an advisor command id:** `memory:save` and `command-memory-save` are now `command-speckit-save`. `command-spec-kit` is now one id per command, such as `command-speckit-plan`. `command-prompt-improver` is now `command-prompt-improve`.\n+- **If you type `/create:skill` or match on an advisor command id:** type `/create:sk-skill` instead. Change any script or saved prompt that names the old command. `memory:save` and `command-memory-save` are now `command-speckit-save`. `command-spec-kit` is now one id per command, such as `command-speckit-plan`. `command-prompt-improver` is now `command-prompt-improve`. `command-create-skill` is now `command-create-sk-skill`.\n - **On Codex:** approve the project hooks in `/hooks`, including the new `SessionStart` row. Until then the advisor brief never arrives. The compaction pointer does not run either. To turn on meaning matching anywhere, run `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder`.\n - **If Skilled is installed in your project:** run `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-hook-registrations.cjs` from the project root. It rewrites `.claude/settings.json` and `.codex/hooks.json`. Add `--check` to check for drift without writing.\n - **If you set a `SYSTEM_SESSION_RELAUNCH_*` or `SESSION_RELAUNCH_*` variable:** rename it in your shell profile or `hook-flags.env` by replacing `SESSION_RELAUNCH` with `SESSION_HANDOVER`. For example, `SYSTEM_SESSION_RELAUNCH_ENABLED` becomes `SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_ENABLED`. The old names are not read. A Claude Code user who had switched handover off gets it back until the new switch is set to 0.\ndiff --git a/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown b/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown\nindex c9671f47710..b0d3c68017f 100644\n--- a/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown\n+++ b/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown\n@@ -99,8 +99,8 @@ The scripts validate route manifests, inspect MCP installations, audit parent sk\n | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | The quality config doctor's entry, kept thin: the arguments, the run context, the one call that starts the runner, the mode table and the exit status are here, and each mode sits in the quality modules under `lib/`. It reports which quality config the sk-code quality gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, and the trust state the runner applies to that config: `trusted`, `untrusted` or `none`. `status` and `check` both print it, and when the config is untrusted they print `git config --local skilled.trustRepoChecks true` as the repair. It prints the hygiene layers, a `Hygiene:` line naming the core rules `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off and an applied-rules block built by calling `quality-gate.cjs --explain` over a sample of one file per first three folders and extension: each checklist with its seat, its override rows or the reasons its section was refused, each same-level conflict and a `Hygiene rules:` line for the rules in force. A refused section, an unknown rule id or a missing checklist fails the status with `CONFIG_INVALID` or `CHECKLIST_MISSING`, and a conflict is listed without failing it. It validates that config and creates `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` once from the shipped template for `/doctor:sk-code quality`, and in a repository with no `specs/` folder its plan suggests the spec-kit hygiene rules without writing them. It never runs a command the config names. It exits 0 on success, 1 on an invalid or missing config, a refused checklist section or a refused init and 2 on a usage error or outside a git repository. |\n | `audit_descriptions.py` | Audits description lengths across skills, commands and agents. |\n | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Verifies every agent in `.skilled/agents` reaches all eight runtime trees, and that the Cursor and Devin entries stay symlinked onto the Claude fork rather than forked. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n-| `agents-md-check.cjs` | The read-only AGENTS.md check: twelve rows reporting the install state, the sections lost or reordered, the currency of each body, every pin, the delivery budget, the shape of the repository block, the repository's own content and its local edits, each runtime copy, the `REPO RULES.md` router and the source template's purity. Three of them run their owner as a child instead of re-deriving it, carrying the rule-copy canary's budget lines, the gate 1 pointer sync's Cursor block and the repo-rules checker's summary. `--root <dir>` classifies another repository and `--json` prints the same report for a machine. A row it cannot prove reads `UNKNOWN` rather than `PASS`, so it exits 0 on no finding, 1 on a finding or an unproven row and 2 on a failed precondition. |\n-| `agents-md-embed.cjs` | The writing half of `/doctor:agents-md`, in three subcommands. `plan` composes every target in memory and prints the targets, the diffs, the new block, the byte budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest, and writes nothing. `apply --plan-digest <digest>` recomposes, refuses a digest the tree no longer produces, backs up every path it will touch, writes only inside the allowlist it checks in code and then proves the composition against the bytes on disk, restoring from the backup and ending `STATUS=FAIL` when one line went missing. `restore --run=<dir>` puts back exactly the paths that run's `manifest.json` records, removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created, and keeps the backup. It exits 0 when there was nothing to do or the work finished, 1 on a failed proof or a declined run and 2 on a failed precondition or a path outside the allowlist. |\n+| `agents-md-check.cjs` | The read-only AGENTS.md check: twelve rows reporting the install state, the sections lost or reordered, the currency of each body, every pin, the delivery budget, the shape of the repository block, the repository's own content and its local edits, each runtime copy, the `REPO RULES.md` router and the source template's purity. Four of them run their owner as a child instead of re-deriving it, carrying the rule-copy canary's budget lines, the gate 1 pointer sync's Cursor block, the repo-rules checker's summary and the manifest generator's check of the source template's purity. `--root <dir>` classifies another repository and `--json` prints the same report for a machine. A row it cannot prove reads `UNKNOWN` rather than `PASS`, so it exits 0 on no finding, 1 on a finding or an unproven row and 2 on a failed precondition. |\n+| `agents-md-embed.cjs` | The writing half of `/doctor:agents-md`, in three subcommands. `plan` composes every target in memory and prints the targets, the diffs, the new block, the byte budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest, and writes nothing. `apply --plan-digest <digest>` recomposes, refuses a digest the tree no longer produces, backs up every path it will touch, writes only inside the allowlist it checks in code and then proves the composition against the bytes on disk, restoring from the backup and ending `STATUS=FAIL` when one line went missing or one appeared outside the block that no shipped section carries. It refuses a symlinked `REPO RULES.md` or Cursor mirror it would write, because a write through the link would change a file the repository does not own. `restore --run=<dir>` puts back exactly the paths that run's `manifest.json` records, removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created, and keeps the backup. It exits 0 when there was nothing to do or the work finished, 1 on a failed proof or a declined run and 2 on a failed precondition or a path outside the allowlist. It exits 3 when a write fails after the backup, having restored from it or said why not. |\n | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | Generates and gates the shipped manifest's `derived` key: a copy of every shipped body, its hash and the router rows, taken from the root documents. `--write` refreshes it and `--check` is the freshness gate, so a body that drifted from the template fails here instead of surfacing as a check finding. It refuses a heading no authored entry matches, an authored entry that matches nothing, and text above the first heading, and `--write` runs only in the repository that ships the template. Each of the three engines has its own suite: `agents-md-check.test.cjs`, `agents-md-embed.test.cjs` and `agents-md-manifest.test.cjs`, driven from the recipe folders under `tests/fixtures/agents-md/`. |\n | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | Lists the shipped hook gates from `scripts/git-hooks/lib/gates.tsv` with their saved `speckit.hooks.<key>` values, and sets or unsets one in local or global git config for `/doctor:git hooks`. Refuses the per-push approvals. |\n | `git-standards.cjs` | Shows where a repository's commit, PR and branch rules come from, copies the shipped sk-git templates into `.sk-git/`, and changes a rules block setting or removes a kind's rules section in those copies for `/doctor:git standards`, re-validating each change with sk-git's own contract check. |\n@@ -109,7 +109,7 @@ The scripts validate route manifests, inspect MCP installations, audit parent sk\n | `doctor-helper-match.cjs` | Ranks the doctor commands of `_routes.yaml` against a problem stated in plain words for `/doctor:helper`: one entry per route, standalone command, standalone action and mcp sub-action, scored by inverse-document-frequency overlap over the manifest phrases plus a bonus for a fully covered phrase, with a preference for the mutation class the operator asked for. Each candidate carries the manifest phrase that best matched and the terms it matched, so the reason on every line is evidence. It skips its own route and every private name, reads one YAML file and writes nothing. It exits 0 on a match, 1 on no candidate above the floor and 2 on a bad argument, an unreadable manifest or a missing YAML parser. |\n | `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` | Scores every activated doctor phrase and each runnable doctor command through the built advisor scorer, then keeps the three results the hooks show. It fails when a phrase reaches neither the top pick nor the second line. It also fails if a runnable command does not rank first or an activated phrase has fewer than three words. It exits 2 when the advisor build is missing or stale. |\n | `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` | The read-only Codex approval check: it finds the advisor's `UserPromptSubmit` entry in `.codex/hooks.json` and reports whether the Codex config holds a `trusted_hash` for that exact group and index, because Codex skips an unapproved entry without a warning. `--root <dir>` reads another checkout, `--config <path>` reads another config and `--json` prints one object. It prints `SKIP no-codex-hooks`, `PASS codex-hooks-approved`, `WARN codex-advisor-hook-absent` or `WARN codex-hooks-unapproved <root>` with the `/hooks` repair line, and never prints a hash. It exits 0 on a skip or an approval, 1 on a missing or unapproved entry and 2 on a bad argument. |\n-| `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | The read-only hook-wiring check: it reads `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json`, `.cursor/hooks.json` and `.devin/hooks.v1.json`, and reports whether the prompt hook entry is present for each runtime folder it finds. `--root <dir>` is required and `--json` prints one object. It prints `ok <runtime>` or `hook-missing <runtime> <file>`, skips a runtime whose folder is absent and never writes. It exits 0 when every installed runtime is wired, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. |\n+| `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | The read-only hook-wiring check: it reads `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json`, `.cursor/hooks.json`, `.devin/hooks.v1.json` and `.vibe/hooks.toml`, and reports whether the prompt hook entry is wired for each runtime folder it finds. An entry counts as wired only when one of its commands, read the way a shell reads it, starts node on that runtime's hook script, so a script named in an `echo` or a comment does not count. It reads `.vibe/hooks.toml` line by line for a `pre_agent` entry that runs the Vibe turn-start hook, because the repository installs no TOML parser. Pi, DSH and OpenCode load their prompt hook through an extension or a plugin, so the check has no row for them. `--root <dir>` is required and `--json` prints one object. It prints `ok <runtime>` or `hook-missing <runtime> <file>`, skips a runtime whose folder is absent and never writes. It exits 0 when every installed runtime is wired, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. |\n | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Checks this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. On 1 it proves plugin discovery through `debug config` and plugin load through an import check. On 2 it starts and stops a private server with the v2 binary it selected (`$OPENCODE_BIN`, `opencode` or `opencode2`, whichever reports a v2 version) and reads plugin and skill state from it. It exits 0 when every requested leg passes, 1 on a failed leg, 2 on a usage error, a crash or an `--out` file it cannot write and 3 when a leg is degraded or skipped. |\n | `ci-report.cjs` | The `/doctor:ci report` entry, kept thin: the flags, the exit codes and every name `/doctor:ci toggle` reads live here, and the readings live in the `ci-report` modules under `lib/`. It reads every workflow under `.github/workflows/` and reports its switch state, triggers, path filters and drift flags for `/doctor:ci report`. A switch is any variable a job's `if:` compares against `off`, so the workflow file is its only copy, and a repository variable value is reduced to a state and never printed. The branch, the required switch, the exempt files, the README and the rebuild trailer come from the CI profile, and the JSON names the profile it read. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 when a workflow fails to parse, 2 on a bad argument and 3 when the CI profile is missing or invalid. |\n | `ci-scaffold.cjs` | Renders the sk-code-ci workflow or job template from the CI profile for `/doctor:ci scaffold`, and with `--protected` prints the profile's protected workflow names. It writes nothing. |\n@@ -232,7 +232,7 @@ On this repository all legs pass on OpenCode 1.19.3 and 2.0.32. OpenCode 1.19.3\n | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | `plan` is read-only. `apply` writes only the paths in its own allowlist, all of them under the target root, after backing each one up under `.skilled/output/doctor-agents-md/<digest>/` (or the OS temp folder when that path is not ignored), and restores from that backup itself when its post-write proof fails. `restore` writes only the paths the run's `manifest.json` records and keeps the backup folder. It never runs `git add`, `git commit` or `git stash`. |\n | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | `--check` is read-only. `--write` rewrites the manifest's `derived` key and refuses to run outside the repository that ships the template. |\n | `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root. It reads `.codex/hooks.json` and the Codex config, prints no hash and takes no write path. It exits 0 on a skip or an approval, 1 on a missing or unapproved entry and 2 on a bad argument. |\n-| `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root. It writes no file and runs no installer, and its JSON form reports the same readings as its lines. It exits 0 when every installed runtime carries its entry, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. |\n+| `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root. It writes no file and runs no installer, and its JSON form reports the same readings as its lines. It exits 0 when every installed runtime's entry runs its hook script, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. Its Vibe reading is line by line, so a `.vibe/hooks.toml` that Vibe cannot parse can still read `ok vibe`, and a value with a trailing comment or a single-quoted string reads `hook-missing`. |\n | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | The battery itself writes only the `--out` file and temporary folders it removes when each step ends. The private server's folder is also removed on `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM`, and the server is stopped. The import check runs each plugin's v1 factory in a child process rooted at the repository, so anything a plugin does when it loads happens during the check. It never touches the shared v2 service and never prints the private server's password. OpenCode 1 writes into a temporary data folder, not the user's. |\n \n Do not invoke a mutating path from a route classified as read-only.\ndiff --git a/.skilled/hooks/shared/session-readers/README.md b/.skilled/hooks/shared/session-readers/README.md\nindex 586fa23f85f..bc58368c3f2 100644\n--- a/.skilled/hooks/shared/session-readers/README.md\n+++ b/.skilled/hooks/shared/session-readers/README.md\n@@ -25,15 +25,16 @@ The six decision-capture sweeps keep their own inline readers and remain the wri\n \n - **Exact id only.** A caller names a runtime and one session id. No reader guesses the newest session or lists a store.\n - **Read-only.** Files are read and SQLite stores are opened with read-only flags. The side-effect suite checks that no read changes a store's bytes or modification time.\n+- **Contained open.** A reader resolves its store path against the runtime's root before opening it, and opens the path its containment check resolved, so a path swapped after the check is not followed.\n - **One envelope.** `readSession` returns `{ ok: true, source, items }` or `{ ok: false, code }`. It never throws across the boundary and never returns a partial list.\n - **Capability is the caller's gate.** `index.mjs` exports `capabilities`, one row per runtime with its `status`, locator kind and store root, plus a `reason` when the runtime is gated. The dispatcher does not consult it. The compaction hooks and the lookup CLI check that a runtime is `ready` before they offer or read history.\n \n-`loadSession` returns the raw neutral records. Claude, Pi, Devin and DSH also return `injected`, a list of `{ source, text }` for context a hook or harness put into the session, which `records` never carries. `loadSession` passes `injected` through and `readSession` ignores it, so only a caller on the raw path sees what the harness added.\n+`loadSession` returns the raw neutral records. Claude, Pi, Devin and DSH also return `injected`, a list of `{ source, text }` for context a hook or harness put into the session, which `records` never carries. `loadSession` passes `injected` through and `readSession` ignores it, so only a caller on the raw path sees what the harness added. A Codex custom tool call has no JSON arguments, so its record carries the call's body as `arguments.input`, a string.\n \n - Claude's `injected` source is `hook_additional_context`, one entry per hook context attachment.\n - Pi's `injected` source is the entry's custom type, such as `session-start-context`, or `custom_message` when it carries none.\n - Devin's `injected` source is `system` for a harness node and `unflagged-user` for a user node the harness wrote. The transcript fallback uses the step's own source.\n-- DSH's `injected` source is the event's source kind, such as `repo-guards`, or `unknown` when it names none.\n+- DSH's `injected` source is the event's source kind, such as `repo-guards`, or `unknown` when it names none. A `user/message` with no source stays injected and never becomes a record.\n \n Failure codes:\n \n@@ -44,7 +45,7 @@ Failure codes:\n | `session_not_found` | No store record matches the id |\n | `session_mismatch` | A record was found, but its recorded id or path does not match |\n | `session_ambiguous` | More than one record claims the id |\n-| `store_unreadable` | The store could not be read or projected |\n+| `store_unreadable` | The store could not be read or projected. For Devin, an open or projection failure falls back to the transcript first, so the code means the transcript failed too |\n | `unsupported_format` | The journal holds no record shape the reader knows, so it fails instead of returning an empty session |\n \n Vibe is gated. Its reader projects `harness.public-session-state/v1` and the completion records beside it, and its helpers are `loadVibeSession`, `lastAssistantText`, `turnToolCalls`, `compactionsSince` and `turnId`. The reply is read from the current turn's completion record first, because Vibe runs a `post_agent` hook before it appends the assistant message to the projection. A hook passes the payload's `transcript_path` (the session storage root); the journal is `<transcript_path>/journal/*.jsonl`. An interactive session folds its journal into a snapshot generation at each quiet point and restarts the journal after it, so the reader starts from the generation `CURRENT` names, its projection chunks under `chunks/`, and applies the journal tail after that generation's `snapshot_sequence`. A compaction folded into a snapshot carries the snapshot's sequence.\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md b/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\nindex fa40840eef3..b22dee1e4e1 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\n@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n name: system-spec-kit\n description: \"Spec-folder workflow + context preservation: Levels 1-3+, validation, trigger-index, ripgrep retrieval. Required for file changes.\"\n allowed-tools: [Bash, Edit, Glob, Grep, Read, Task, Write]\n-version: 2.13.3.0\n+version: 2.13.4.0\n ---\n \n <!-- Keywords: spec-kit, speckit, documentation-workflow, spec-folder, template-enforcement, context-preservation, progressive-documentation, validation, trigger-index, retrieval-conventions, ripgrep-retrieval, continuity-writer, handover, opencode-goal, goal-plugin, active_goal, session-goal, importance-tiers -->\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff 695f4191d36620b42b5f76a28c25e460fccdd0f7 85550ee946e -- .github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml .skilled/agents/markdown.md .skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/sk-doc/manifest.json .skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown .skilled/hooks/shared/session-readers/README.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794815619232,"end":1794815619364}}}}
