shared field vocabulary that `/create:sk-skill`, `/create:skill-parent`, `/doctor:skill-advisor tune`, and `/doctor:skill-advisor parent-skill` all render after touching a skill root, so that scaffolding a skill and diagnosing its advisor state describe the same reality in the same words.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/shared/advisor-index-handoff.md:116:| Parent `/create:skill-parent` create | Full handoff, H-specific values (`leaf-manifest.json` freshness from the scoped generator this workflow just ran) |\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/shared/advisor-index-handoff.md:117:| Parent `/create:skill-parent` update | Full handoff, H-specific values |\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:277:The operator plans the conversion of a declared hub, compares the tree, then plans a fixture that nests a packet with no declaration. Each refusal names its own reason, and the hub refusal ends at the `/create:skill-parent` command line rather than a write.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/manual-testing-playbook/standalone-skill/refuse-a-hub-candidate.md:15:This scenario validates the refusal path of the conversion. A skill that declares a hub, or that nests packet `SKILL.md` files, is a hub candidate. Convert names the reason, writes nothing and points at `/create:skill-parent`.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/manual-testing-playbook/standalone-skill/refuse-a-hub-candidate.md:31:- Expected signals: the declared hub prints `HUB_DECLARATION: Hub declaration mode-registry.json is present; conversion does not modify hubs.` and exits 1. The nested-packet fixture, which carries no declaration, prints `NESTED_PACKETS: Nested SKILL.md packets make this a hub candidate.` and exits 1. A `local-` prefixed name prints `RESERVED_PREFIX` and an `agent-` prefixed one prints `PERSONA_PREFIX`, each with no write. The tree listing is byte-identical after each attempt. Through the command, the run ends with `STATUS=FAIL` and the same reason, followed by `Next: /create:skill-parent <the parent skill folder>`.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/manual-testing-playbook/standalone-skill/refuse-a-hub-candidate.md:45:| SKL-012 | Refuse a hub candidate | Refuse a declared hub and a nested packet tree with a named reason and no write | `Convert the field-notes skill into the Skilled format. Both of its modes share one folder, so convert them as one skill.` | 1. `bash: ls .skilled/skills/field-notes/mode-registry.json` -> 2. `bash: node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs plan field-notes` -> 3. `bash: git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --porcelain .skilled/skills/field-notes` -> 4. `bash: ls .skilled/skills/field-notes-nested/modes/fixture-mode/SKILL.md` -> 5. `bash: node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs plan field-notes-nested` -> 6. `bash: git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --porcelain .skilled/skills/field-notes-nested` | Step 1: the declaration is present and there is no nested packet. Step 2: `HUB_DECLARATION: Hub declaration mode-registry.json is present; conversion does not modify hubs.`, exit 1. Step 3: no output. Step 4: the second fixture nests a packet and declares no hub. Step 5: `NESTED_PACKETS: Nested SKILL.md packets make this a hub candidate.`, exit 1. Step 6: no output | The prompt, both fixtures, both refusal lines with their exit statuses, the unchanged tree listings and the handoff line the command prints after the refusal | PASS if each candidate prints its own named reason, nothing changes and the hub refusal names `/create:skill-parent`. FAIL if any attempt writes, two cases share one reason, or a hub candidate is converted | 1. When the reason is `NESTED_PACKETS`, find the nested `SKILL.md` and confirm the folder is meant to be a hub. 2. When it is `HUB_DECLARATION`, read the named declaration rather than deleting it. 3. When it is `RESERVED_PREFIX` or `PERSONA_PREFIX`, rename the skill instead